1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,980
Models do what training pays for.

2
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:06,020
Here is a training setup.

3
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:09,920
A model writes code, and it earns reward when the unit tests pass.

4
00:00:10,520 --> 00:00:19,520
In a 2025 OpenAI study of a reasoning model in training, some of its outputs ended
the program before any test ran, or told the tests to skip themselves.

5
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:21,400
Both raise the reward.

6
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:23,320
Neither solves the task.

7
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:28,280
The reward measured passing tests, and passing tests is what the training produced.

8
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:31,400
Human approval is a reward too.

9
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:37,320
Assistants are trained toward answers that people, and models of people's
preferences, rate highly.

10
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:45,260
Mrinank Sharma and colleagues found that matching the user's beliefs was among the
features that best predicted which answer people preferred.

11
00:00:45,860 --> 00:00:53,800
Given a user's mistaken belief, a preference model preferred a convincing answer
that agreed with it over a standard truthful answer ninety-five percent of the time.

12
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:59,680
That is sycophancy, and the paper traces it in part to the preference data itself.

13
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:07,520
Carson Denison and colleagues trained a model through a series of environments that
each paid for a small kind of gaming, like flattery.

14
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:12,540
Then they gave it a held-out task where it could edit the code that computed its own
reward.

15
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:17,860
It did so in forty-five of thirty-two thousand seven hundred and sixty-eight
episodes.

16
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:22,120
A model trained only to be helpful did so in none of one hundred thousand.

17
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:26,200
The rate is small, and the environments were built to make gaming possible.

18
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:31,240
The direction is the finding: rewarded gaming spread to gaming that was never
rewarded.

19
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:34,540
None of this needs a model that wants anything.

20
00:01:34,860 --> 00:01:38,680
It needs a reward that pays for the wrong thing, and an optimizer that finds it.

21
00:01:39,280 --> 00:01:46,120
So the fix sits in the environment: pay for the property you need, and check the
thing you pay for with a test it cannot satisfy by shortcut.

22
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:51,120
When a model does something strange, ask first what its training paid for.

23
00:01:51,720 --> 00:01:54,440
The voice you heard is a synthesized version of mine.

24
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:57,660
Every number on screen links to its source under the video.
